# Python
build/
dist/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/

# Output of a real run. Every one of these is somebody's corpus: PMIDs, author
# names, affiliations, and — if the two optional tables were used — a named list
# of one advisor's graduates. None of it belongs in a public repository, and the
# default output directory is `pubmed_results/` right next to the code, so this
# is the line that keeps an ordinary run from becoming a commit.
pubmed_results/
papers_*.json
citations_*.json
journal_worklist_*.csv
advisor_profile_*.html
advisor_profile_*.md
advisor_profile_*.json
advisor_compare_*.md
advisor_compare_*.json
*.xlsx
paper_cache.db
pdfs/
*.log

# The two dated files the network verbs write, added when those verbs were.
# Same reason as the block above: each names real journals and real papers from
# somebody's corpus, and `impact_reference_*.json` additionally carries a
# per-paper position that only means anything next to the corpus it came from.
journal_risk_*.json
impact_reference_*.json

# The four tables a user fills in by hand. They are that user's work and may
# carry licensed figures typed from a vendor page.
#
# `evaluations*.csv` is the one to be most careful with: it holds third-party
# statements about a named individual, collected by hand from public pages. It
# is the user's research notes, it is about someone who did not publish it here,
# and it has no business in a public repository under any circumstances.
journal_table*.csv
thesis_roster*.csv
theses*.csv
evaluations*.csv
evaluation_table*.csv
chinese_records*.csv
cnki*.csv

# Local config: may carry an NCBI API key and an email address.
config.json

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
